`RepoDdg` carries `forfeit_refutation`, the set of symbol ids whose CFG
statement extents do not cover every call node in their body (or whose language
declares no `call_node_types`, which forfeits for the same reason).
`build_repo_ddg` populates it, `_build_ddg_for_verify_claims` threads it,
`propagate_taint_ddg` consults it.

ONE ARM ONLY, AND THE CHOICE IS THE POINT. The §3a arm tests
`_ddg_taint_reaches(...) is True`, so `False` and `None` already collapse there
and the gate could not alter it — wiring it there would be theatre. On the
barrier arm a `False` earns `sanitized`, and a sanitized flow is DROPPED from
the claim's violation set, so a `False` from a function the extractor did not
fully see suppresses a real violation. That arm got the gate.

DIRECTION: strictly fewer suppressions, hence strictly more surviving
violations. That asymmetry is why this can land before removal authority exists.

KEYED ON THE SOURCE FUNCTION, not consulted globally — pinned by its own test,
because an implementation that forfeited whenever the set was merely non-empty
would pass the headline test while silently suppressing every sanitizer in the
repo the moment one function anywhere was uncovered.

MEASURED DELTA: ZERO. alertmanager yields 59 evidence rows, all `ddg_mixed`,
0 sanitized. The gate can only turn `sanitized` into not-sanitized, so with no
sanitized findings it provably changed nothing. Reported as a CLOSED ROUTE, not
an improvement — the same shape WI-votom landed in. It also independently
corroborates INV-karud clause (b)'s "0 registered sanitizer call sites across
95,950 taint call edges" on fresh substrate: the shipped sanitizer vocabulary is
crypto-only (go 1, python 4, rust 3, typescript 3, javascript 0). 445 of
alertmanager's 1,329 walkable functions DO forfeit, so the route is closed
before anything could walk it wrongly — the order WI-joluk demands.

CROSS-CHECK: production `build_repo_ddg` reproduces the standalone instrument's
counts exactly (445 of 1329), so the measurement and the shipped path agree
rather than being two implementations of one idea.

THE FORFEIT POPULATION DIFFERS BY LANGUAGE, AND WAS READ RATHER THAN ASSUMED.
Go forfeits on the `if err := do(); err != nil` initializer its own
`cfg_nodes/go.yaml` self-documents. PYTHON FORFEITS ON A DIFFERENT SHAPE
ENTIRELY — the call in a `for x in <call>` iterable and in a `with <call> as f`
context-manager header. A first non-vacuity test reasoned by analogy from Go and
used a call inside an `if` test; that forfeits NOTHING in Python, because the
`if` condition contains its calls. Hence 12.8% (Python) against 33.5% (Go): the
languages fail in different places. This is the strongest evidence yet that
coverage-gating rather than gap-enumeration was the right design — a gap list
would have shipped with Go's case handled and Python's invisible.

Note for the escape-reclassification work: those Python sites are GENUINE
coverage gaps, not misclassified escapes. `for x in <call>` and
`with <call> as f` both BIND a value, so data really does flow where the DDG
cannot see. They are not candidates for the expression-read reclassification,
which targets assert/binary/return/defer where nothing is stored.

